        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful, idiomatic mirror of the established validation-gate pattern already in this pipeline:\n\n- `ValidateDailyOwnerLimit` matches its sibling `ValidateCityCapacity` exactly \u2014 same `service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto, OwnerRepository)` signature, same `DAILY_LIMIT` constant style, same read-only \"count then escalate, publish nothing\" shape. A maintainer who knows `ValidateCityCapacity` will read this instantly.\n- `DailyOwnerLimitException` / `DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler` / the escalation YAML are structurally identical to the `CityAtCapacity*` trio (checked exception, `ResponseEntity<Void>` with the status, `handle(@Parameter \u2026)`), differing only in the HTTP status \u2014 correctly 429.\n- Pipeline placement is right: inserted as its own `dailyLimit` step after `cityCapacity` and before `applyHousehold`/`build`, keeping it a pure read-only gate that runs before any entity is built or persisted, consistent with the other gates and their comments.\n- Logic sits in the correct unit (one small function class, one public method, one escalation per concern); nothing is duplicated into a catch-all or bolted onto an existing class.\n\nThe `findAll()`-and-count approach matches the existing `ValidateCityCapacity` precedent exactly, so even though a repository count query might be leaner, following the established house style is the more idiomatic, coherent choice here \u2014 and efficiency/correctness are out of scope for this review regardless."
